Where the experimental value is the value obtained through experimentation, the theoretical value is the value obtained through mathematical calculation, and the absolute value is taken to ensure that the error is always positive.

For example, let's say you calculated the theoretical value for an experiment to be 10, but the experimental value you obtained was 9.5. You can calculate the percent error as follows:

percent error = (|9.5 - 10| / 10) x 100%
percent error = (0.5 / 10) x 100%
percent error = 5%

This means that the experimental value has an error of 5% compared to the theoretical value.

If you have a series of values to compare, you can use statistical measures such as the mean absolute error (MAE), root mean squared error (RMSE), or coefficient of determination (R-squared) to assess the difference between the calculated and experimental values.

1. Mean Absolute Error (MAE)
The MAE is the average of the absolute differences between the calculated and experimental values. It gives a measure of the average magnitude of the errors.

MAE = (1/n) \* Σ|y\_exp - y\_calc|

where y\_exp is the experimental value, y\_calc is the calculated value, and n is the total number of values being compared.

1. Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)
The RMSE is the square root of the average of the squared differences between the calculated and experimental values. It gives a measure of the standard deviation of the errors.

RMSE = √[(1/n) \* Σ(y\_exp - y\_calc)^2]

1. Coefficient of Determination (R-squared)
The coefficient of determination (R-squared) measures the proportion of the variance in the experimental values that can be explained by the calculated values. It gives a measure of the goodness of fit of the calculated values to the experimental data.

R-squared = 1 - (Σ(y\_exp - y\_calc)^2 / Σ(y\_exp - ȳ)^2)

where ȳ is the mean of the experimental values.

These measures can be used individually or in combination to assess the accuracy of the calculated values compared to the experimental data.

To compare a series of theoretical lens powers with another series of lens powers calculated using ray tracing software, you can use the statistical measures I mentioned in the previous response.

For example, you can calculate the mean absolute error (MAE) between the two series of values as follows:

1. Calculate the absolute difference between each theoretical value and the corresponding ray tracing value.
2. Take the average of the absolute differences to get the mean absolute error.

MAE = (1/n) \* Σ|P\_theo - P\_rt|

where P\_theo is the theoretical lens power, P\_rt is the lens power obtained from ray tracing software, and n is the total number of values being compared.

Similarly, you can calculate the root mean squared error (RMSE) and coefficient of determination (R-squared) to assess the difference between the two series of values.

RMSE = √[(1/n) \* Σ(P\_theo - P\_rt)^2]

R-squared = 1 - (Σ(P\_theo - P\_rt)^2 / Σ(P\_theo - ȳ)^2)

where ȳ is the mean of the theoretical lens powers.

These measures can help you assess the accuracy of the ray tracing software in calculating the lens power compared to the theoretical values.

The value of the Mean Absolute Error (MAE) represents the average magnitude of the differences between the theoretical lens powers and the lens powers calculated using ray tracing software.

The MAE value is expressed in the same unit as the lens powers being compared. A smaller MAE value indicates a better agreement between the theoretical and ray tracing lens powers, while a larger MAE value indicates a larger disagreement or error.

To put the MAE value into context, you can compare it with the range of the lens powers being compared. For example, if the range of the lens powers is from 0 to 10 diopters, an MAE of 0.2 diopters may be considered small, whereas an MAE of 2 diopters may be considered large.

In general, the MAE value alone cannot determine whether the ray tracing software is accurate or not, but it can give you an idea of the level of agreement between the theoretical and calculated lens powers. You can also use other statistical measures such as the Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) and the Coefficient of Determination (R-squared) to further evaluate the accuracy of the ray tracing software.

